remote: how winch reaches a host and runs there

winch reaches each host over SSH and starts a helper there. All work on
a host goes through the helper.

Sessions:
  winch uses the OpenSSH client of the controller and its SSH config.
  The address and user of the inventory override it, and --user
  overrides those. A session is one ssh process with the helper at the
  other end. It opens at the first work of its host and stays open
  across stages. It closes when its host has no more work, and at the
  apply question.

  winch passes ConnectTimeout=15, ServerAliveInterval=15, and
  ServerAliveCountMax=3, so a host that does not answer fails and does
  not hang the run. It also passes RemoteCommand=none,
  PermitLocalCommand=no, ClearAllForwardings=yes, ForwardAgent=no, and
  ForwardX11=no, so the SSH config does not replace or add to the
  command of winch.

Questions from ssh:
  With a terminal, ssh can ask for a new host key or a key passphrase.
  Sessions then start one at a time in inventory order, so each
  question belongs to one host. Without a terminal, or with --batch,
  winch passes BatchMode=yes, and sessions start in parallel. A host
  that needs a question is then unreachable. The access check and the
  connections after a reboot always use BatchMode=yes.

Failed connections:
  When the host closes the connection before the SSH handshake, as a
  busy sshd does when it reaches MaxStartups, winch tries once more
  after 2 seconds. Any other failure, such as a timeout, a refused
  login, or a missing sudo, fails the host at once. So a retry never
  counts as a second failed login.

The login user:
  The login user is root, or has passwordless sudo: winch starts the
  helper as root, through sudo -n for another user. When sudo fails,
  the error shows the message of sudo. The shell of the login user is
  a POSIX shell, and prints nothing on stdout before the script of
  winch starts.

The helper:
  The helper is a static binary for linux/amd64 or linux/arm64. A short
  script looks for it in /var/lib/winch/bin/ by its SHA-256 hash. When
  it is missing, or its hash differs, winch sends it through the same
  session. The helper deletes each other file in bin/ that is older
  than 30 days.

  A host needs sh, id, uname, head, ls, and sha256sum. /var/lib/winch
  and its bin/ are owned by root, and others cannot write to them. The
  file system of /var/lib/winch allows running programs: it is not
  mounted noexec.

  A check of file content sends only its hash. Apply sends the content
  when the hashes differ.

The lock:
  The helper takes a lock in /var/lib/winch/. A second session on the
  same host fails. The error names the run that holds the lock and the
  process ID of its helper, so you can end a helper that its run left
  behind.

Commands on the host:
  Every command on the host, also one that a module runs, such as
  apt-get or systemctl, runs as root in /, with this environment:

    LC_ALL  C.UTF-8
    PATH    /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    HOME    /root
    umask   022

  LANGUAGE and the variables that sudo adds are not set. A cmd with dir
  runs in that directory. A cmd with user runs as that user, with the
  home of that user as HOME. It starts no login session: PAM does not
  run, so the login umask and limits of the user do not apply.

The time limit:
  A command stops after 10 minutes, or after the timeout of a cmd
  step. The helper sends SIGTERM to the command and its child
  processes, and SIGKILL 10 seconds later to those that still run. The
  step fails. apt-get in pkg and upgrade has no time limit.

  When the controller goes away, the helper lets the running command
  finish within its limit, and then exits. The command of winch exec
  gets SIGINT, and SIGKILL after 10 seconds.

State on the host:
  bin/                     the helper
  handlers/<project>       pending handlers
  downloads/<project>      checksum, owner, and group of the last unpack
  then/<project>           pending then commands
  certbot-hooks/<project>  pending certbot hooks
  checks/<project>         pending health gates and asserts
  firewall.list            the firewall table after its last load
  rollback/                old files for confirm_access, during a run
  reboot                   the boot ID and the error of a failed reboot
  lock                     the session lock

  These paths are in /var/lib/winch/. Each record file in handlers/,
  downloads/, then/, certbot-hooks/, and checks/ starts with its format
  version. A version newer than winch knows fails the host: upgrade
  winch to read <file>.

Example:
  # ~/.ssh/config
  Host *.acme.com
    Port 2222
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519

  # inventories/production.yaml
  hosts:
    web1.acme.com:
      user: admin
